{
  "version": 1,
  "description": "The platforms a business takes bookings or orders at instead of its inbox (doors_elsewhere, spec 0.9.0, ADR-031). id is the token a manifest's doors_elsewhere entry names as platform, and the name a network gives such a door as its type platform:<id>. A URL is on a platform when its host is one of hosts or a name under one. It is the business's own page there when one of the platform's pages patterns matches (matched without regard to case against the host without www. followed by the path), and, for a platform with no pages, when it is past the platform's own names: a name under the host in reserved_subdomains, or a first path segment in reserved_paths, is the platform's own; then page path asks for a path other than /, page subdomain for a name under the host other than www, page any for either. own_site: the platform also serves the business's pages on the business's own domain; a page there is named by its host, and carries the platform's token only when its host is on the business's own website (the manifest's profile.url). hosts were read from each platform's own public pages and help (source), without signing in. Not listed: delivery apps and stay marketplaces (Uber Eats, DoorDash, Deliveroo, Booking.com, Airbnb, Expedia…). Some listed platforms charge the business per booking or order; that is between the business and the platform. A business may still name any https page of its own choosing, with no platform. tracking_params are the query parameters a door may not carry: a name ending in * is a prefix. Generic names a platform may use on its own direct link (ref, partner…) are not among them.",
